{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MUbXGm9UJ45vdAHivgPzLG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as a test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/sys/` - Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Shared output formatting\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current State of Implementation**: Several packages already have implementations in place—the testutils package has its differential testing harness, the sys package has syscall abstractions for Darwin and Linux with signal and terminal handling, and the format package has color output support.\n\nLooking at the command implementations, I see that cat, du, sponge, wc, and version all have their main logic and test coverage already written. However, there's a discrepancy in the release tracking—rel00.0 is marked as code not started, but the source code clearly shows these implementations exist, so the status needs updating. I'm seeing that several releases have code already implemented in the source directory even though they're marked as not started—wc, cat, sponge, and du all have existing implementations. Meanwhile, rel02.0 and beyond are still pending, covering utilities like true, false, yes, basename, dirname, tee, and head. There's a constraint about Go files in source_code that I need to check against the task generation rules. I see there are orphaned PRDs for true and false already written, so those specs exist even though they're not properly linked in the roadmap. Given the constraint of proposing just one task and focusing on rel02.0, I should pick the highest-priority command from that release to implement.\n\nThe pipeline requires PRD → use case → test suite → code, and since prd020-true and prd021-false already exist as orphaned specs, I could anchor a task to one of those. But I'm realizing I don't have the actual content of those PRDs in the project context, which makes it hard to ground the requirements without exploring the filesystem—something I'm explicitly told not to do. I'm realizing I need the actual requirement IDs from prd020-true to properly anchor this task, but since the PRD exists and the stitch agent can read it, I can propose implementing cmd/true with required_reading pointing to that file—it's the simplest possible utility, just exiting with code 0. A PRD for `true` would have:\n- R1: Exit with code 0 (core behavior)\n- R2: Handle --help and --version flags\n- R3: Ignore all arguments\n- R4: SIGPIPE handling\n\nActually, I need to be more careful. Let me think about what I know for certain.\n\nFor `true`, the GNU implementation:\n1. Exits 0 when called with no args\n2. Has --help and --version\n3. Ignores all other arguments\n4. Handles SIGPIPE\n\nFor `false`, the GNU implementation:\n1. Exits 1 when called with no args\n2. Has --help and --version  \n3. Ignores all other arguments\n4. Handles SIGPIPE\n\nThese are essentially identical programs differing only in exit code (and program name in --help/--version output).\n\nNow I'm running into a constraint conflict: the line limit suggests combining trivial changes into one task, but the policy requires splitting implementation and tests separately. Since `true` is so minimal—probably 30-50 lines for implementation and another 30-50 for tests—I should propose just the implementation of cmd/true as a single task, following the split requirement.\n\nI'll draft a task that references the expected PRD requirements for `true` and structure it like the existing cmd/version pattern with main.go and unit tests in a separate task. I'm uncertain about the exact requirement IDs in the PRD for `true`, so rather than guessing at the specifics, I should reference it generically and let the stitch agent handle the implementation details based on whatever requirements are actually documented there. I see that cmd/version already exists and all code through rel01.3 is done. For rel02.0, prd020-true exists but the PRDs for yes, basename, and dirname are still pending. Since I'm limited to exactly one task, I'll focus on implementing cmd/true—I'll reference prd020-true in the required reading and base the requirements on what the standard true command does, letting the stitch agent validate against the actual PRD. Now I'm drafting the task title to include the requirement ID as specified.\n\nLooking at the existing patterns in cmd/version and other utilities, I can see they all follow a consistent structure: they define a progName constant, parse arguments with a dedicated function, install a SIGPIPE handler, use buffered output, and handle standard flags like --version while rejecting unrecognized options with exit code 2. For true specifically, it's simpler—it just exits 0 always, accepts --help and --version, ignores all other arguments without erroring, and handles SIGPIPE. I'll write requirements that are general enough to be correct based on true's actual behavior but specific enough to guide implementation while staying consistent with the project's established patterns. Looking at the existing cmd/ package patterns, I see they all follow a consistent structure with main.go and test files, use SIGPIPE handlers, manual flag parsing, and bufio for output with standardized error formatting. For the `true` command specifically, it needs to exit with 0 regardless of input, handle --help and --version flags, and ignore all other arguments since GNU true accepts anything without error. The `true` command is incredibly simple—just an exit code of 0, with optional --help and --version handling. Looking at the existing utilities, none of them implement those flags, so I'll keep it minimal. Since `true` and `false` are nearly identical except for their exit codes, I could combine them into a single task that covers both, which fits better with the constraint of keeping tasks to at most 4 requirements.\n\nLet me structure this as one task covering both utilities: `true` exits with 0, `false` exits with 1, both handle SIGPIPE properly, and both follow the project's manual argument parsing conventions. That's 4 requirements total and keeps the combined implementation well under 250 lines—probably around 60-80 lines for both main.go files. I need to anchor this to the specific PRD IDs though, so I'll reference prd020-true and prd021-false explicitly in the task definition.\n\nActually, I'm overcomplicating this. Let me just propose implementing cmd/true as the first task since it's the earliest pending work in the earliest incomplete release. I'll structure it as a YAML task with the title, dependency, and description fields, anchoring it to prd020-true and including the required reading section.\n\nFormatting task definition... D for true utility)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component, design decisions DD6 and DD7)\n      - cmd/version/main.go (simplest existing utility -- pattern reference)\n      - pkg/sys/signal.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/true/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Go implementation of true utility matching GNU true behavior under LC_ALL=C\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement cmd/true to exit with status code 0 per prd020-true\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Install SIGPIPE handler via sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler per prd020-true and project conventions\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Handle --help and --version flags if required by prd020-true, following cmd/version patterns\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Ignore all unrecognized arguments without error, matching GNU true behavior per prd020-true\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the manual argument parsing pattern used by cmd/cat, cmd/wc, and cmd/version -- do not use the flag package\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at program start, matching all existing cmd/ packages\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"All differential tests run with LC_ALL=C per ARCHITECTURE.yaml DD6\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/true/main.go compiles with go build ./ and passes all differential tests\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Running the binary with no arguments exits with code 0\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Running the binary with arbitrary arguments exits with code 0\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"All requirements from prd020-true are addressed in the implementation\"\n\nI'm realizing I'm making up requirements that aren't explicitly in the PRD. The constraint says I shouldn't invent design decisions not derived from the PRD or architecture. R3 is conditional on what the PRD says, which isn't ideal. Let me drop the conditional language and focus on what I know for certain: true exits with 0, handles SIGPIPE like other utilities, and ignores arguments. I should let the stitch agent read the actual PRD to fill in details about --help and --version. that and focus on what I'm certain about.\n\nActually, looking at the existing implementations more carefully:\n- None of them implement --help or --version (except cmd/version which IS the version utility)\n- GNU true in coreutils does support --help and --version, but other utilities in this project skip those flags\n\nSo the PRD for true might or might not include --help/--version. I should let the stitch agent handle this by reading the PRD.\n\nLet me simplify and lay out the task structure. I need to specify the required reading (the PRD itself, the architecture docs for design decisions, and cmd/version as a reference), the file to create, and the core requirements: implementing everything in the PRD, exiting with status 0, installing the SIGPIPE handler following the pattern used in other cmd/ utilities, and matching GNU true behavior under LC_ALL=C. conventions\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Follow the project's manual argument parsing pattern (no flag package) and cmd/ package structure conventions established in cmd/version and cmd/cat\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the manual argument parsing pattern used by cmd/cat, cmd/wc, and cmd/version -- do not use the flag package\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at program start, matching all existing cmd/ packages\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Program name constant (progName) follows the pattern from cmd/cat, cmd/wc, cmd/du\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/true/main.go exists and compiles with go build ./cmd/true/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Running the binary with no arguments exits with code 0\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Running the binary with arbitrary arguments exits with code 0 (GNU true ignores all arguments)\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"All requirements from prd020-true.yaml are addressed in the implementation\"\n\nI'm thinking through what GNU true actually does at its core. It essentially calls exit with success status, and the standard behavior includes handling --help and --version flags through the gnulib framework, while ignoring everything else. The PRD probably specifies these standard behaviors along with the core requirement that the program always exits successfully.\n\nSince I don't have the actual PRD content, making R1 a catch-all for \"implement all requirements from prd020-true.yaml\" is the right move—the stitch agent will have access to that document. My more specific requirements capture what must be true regardless: the exit code behavior, the argument handling, and adherence to the project's established patterns for manual argument parsing and SIGPIPE handling.\n\nLooking at the spec status, the PRD exists as an orphan but the use case and test suite probably haven't been created yet. Given the project's principle that PRDs come before use cases and test suites, I should probably be proposing documentation tasks for those instead of jumping straight to code. The spec pipeline is marked pending, which suggests the whole chain needs to be established first.\n\nEven though the `true` command is trivially simple—just exit 0—the methodology still requires following the proper sequence. I need to check what format and sections are required for a use case deliverable so I can propose the right task.\n\nBut actually, I'm realizing the stitch agent is specifically a code generation pipeline, and documentation tasks belong to the human operator during the specification phase. So proposing a use case task wouldn't fit the architecture—I should be working within what the stitch agent can execute.\n\nThe real tension is that P5 requires test suites and use cases before code, but if those specs don't exist yet, should I still propose code tasks? Looking at the existing codebase, everything through rel01.3 is already generated, so the only open work is in rel02.0 and beyond. The PRDs for rel02.0 exist but the use cases are incomplete, so I need to figure out whether to propose code tasks anyway or wait for the specs to be finalized.\n\nI think the answer is to propose the code task for cmd/true. The PRD contains enough specification for the stitch agent to implement from, and looking at how other utilities reference PRD requirements directly in their code, that pattern seems to work. Plus DD7 clarifies that the generation pipeline only targets cmd/ and pkg/ directories anyway, not documentation. I should split this into two tasks though -- one for the implementation and one for the tests, following P2's guidance.\n\nLooking at the existing naming patterns across the codebase, the test file would be cmd/true/true_test.go to match how other commands structure their tests.\n\nNow I'm setting up the task definition with the PRD, architecture docs, and reference implementations as required reading, plus the specific files that need to be created. action: create\nnote: \"Go implementation of GNU true utility, matching reference binary behavior under LC_ALL=C\"\n\nrequirements:\n  - id: R1\n    text: \"Implement the core true behavior: exit with status code 0 when invoked with no arguments, per prd020-true\"\n  - id: R2\n    text: \"Install SIGPIPE handler via sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at program start, matching the convention established by all existing cmd/ packages (cmd/cat, cmd/wc, cmd/du, cmd/sponge, cmd/version)\"\n  - id: R3\n    text: \"Handle all flags and arguments specified in prd020-true.yaml, using the project's manual argument parsing pattern (not the flag standard library package)\"\n  - id: R4\n    text: \"Match GNU true exit code behavior for all argument combinations per prd020-true\"\n\ndesign_decisions:\n  - id: D1\n    text: \"Use manual argument parsing (range over os.Args[1:]) following the pattern in cmd/cat parseArgs and cmd/version parseArgs -- do not use Go's flag package\"\n  - id: D2\n    text: \"Declare a progName constant set to \\\"true\\\", following the convention in cmd/cat, cmd/wc, cmd/du, and cmd/sponge\"\n  - id: D3\n    text: \"Use pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() as the first statement in main(), matching all existing cmd/ packages\"\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    text: \"cmd/true/main.go exists and compiles successfully with 'go build ./cmd/true/'\"\n  - id: AC2\n    text: \"The binary exits with status 0 for all valid invocations matching GNU true behavior\"\n  - id: AC3\n    text: \"All behavioral requirements from prd020-true.yaml are correctly implemented\"\n\nI'm verifying this against the project constraints: no external tools, no issue tracker interaction, no duplicate issues, and this is a new package since cmd/true doesn't exist yet. The task is focused and well-scoped.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":86427,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":86427},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4f84045-568b-4440-ac62-09d444425d0a","uuid":"2686e944-aec3-4140-b236-37a8af1657c7"}
